  title: Sequence encoding of run histories

  subtitle: Paper 5 in a series on UK All-Weather racing outcomes

  author: Patrick Gillen

  date: '2026-09-08'

  abstract: |

    Papers 1 to 3 gave a model of UK All-Weather Flat handicaps a horse's

    recent form as a handful of hand-built numbers: last time out's finishing

    position if it was in the first four, the position two runs back on the

    same rule, whether the horse had ever won, and a small going-affinity

    block. This paper changes how that history reaches the model. The

    summaries are removed and a gated recurrent encoder reads the horse's

    twenty most recent prior runs directly, eight channels a run, with the

    raw finishing position rather than a top-four code. Everything else is

    held fixed: the same races, the same depth-three Plackett–Luce

    likelihood, the same downstream scorer, the same seed. On

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); u <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_race_universe, store = S); format(u$n_races[u$source == "common"], big.mark = ",") })`

    held-out test races the encoder beats the hand-built summaries on every

    ranking measure — P1_rank

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); d <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_contrasts, store = S); v <- d$diff_point[grepl("rung 3b.*rung 1", d$contrast) & d$metric == "P1_rank"]; formatC(v, format = "e", digits = 3, flag = "+") })`,

    Brier_place

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); d <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_contrasts, store = S); v <- d$diff_point[grepl("rung 3b.*rung 1", d$contrast) & d$metric == "Brier_place"]; formatC(v, format = "e", digits = 3) })`,

    pseudo-$R^2$

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); d <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_contrasts, store = S); v <- d$diff_point[grepl("rung 3b.*rung 1", d$contrast) & d$metric == "test_pl_r2"]; formatC(v, format = "e", digits = 3, flag = "+") })`,

    every interval excluding zero — and beats paper 3's gradient-boosted

    trees by more. Three models now separate cleanly on a test split where

    papers 1 to 3 could not be told apart. Two further findings come from the

    ladder that got there: a multi-layer perceptron on identical features

    beat a linear scorer, which paper 3's function-class conclusion did not

    anticipate; and learned entity embeddings for trainer, jockey and sire

    lost to the strike rates they replaced, both intervals excluding zero.

    The market still ranks better than any of it, and no betting return is

    distinguishable from no improvement at all.

  







