  title: Sequence encoding of run histories

  subtitle: Paper 5 in a series on UK All-Weather racing outcomes

  author: Patrick Gillen

  date: '2026-09-08'

  abstract: |

    The models in this series score a horse on 26 features. Eight of them

    describe the horse's own run history, and papers 1 to 3 gave those eight

    as hand-built summaries: last time out's finishing position if it was in

    the first four, the position two runs back on the same rule, whether the

    horse had ever won, and a small going-affinity block. This paper replaces

    those eight with a gated recurrent encoder that reads the horse's twenty

    most recent prior runs directly, eight values a run, with the raw

    finishing position rather than a top-four code. The other eighteen

    features — official rating, weight, age, sex, headgear, draw, course,

    going, the layoff since the last run, and the trainer, jockey and sire

    strike rates — are unchanged, as are the races, the depth-three

    Plackett–Luce likelihood and the downstream scorer. On

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); u <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_race_universe, store = S); format(u$n_races[u$source == "common"], big.mark = ",") })`

    held-out test races the encoder beats the hand-built features on every

    ranking measure, every interval excluding zero, and beats paper 3's

    gradient-boosted trees by more; Section 1 gives the figures. Three models

    now separate cleanly on a test split where papers 1 to 3 could not be told

    apart. Two further findings: a multi-layer perceptron on identical

    features beat a linear scorer, which paper 3's conclusion about function

    class did not anticipate; and embeddings — a short list of numbers the

    model learns for each trainer, jockey and sire — lost to the strike rates

    they replaced, both intervals excluding zero. The market still ranks better than any of these models.

    The single-bet win backtest improves to

    `r local({ S <- file.path(rprojroot::find_root(rprojroot::has_file("awracing.Rproj")), "_targets_p5"); b <- targets::tar_read(p5_test_backtests, store = S); sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * b$roi[b$arm == "rung3b" & b$bet == "win"]) })`,

    the best in the series, but the difference from paper 3 cannot be

    distinguished from zero.

  







